From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Miha Kralj <miha.kralj@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 19:50:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: sfp: tolerate known-bad EEPROM checksum on generic
 OEM 10G DAC

Generic no-name "OEM" 10G passive DAC modules (vendor_pn
"SFP-H10GB-CU1M") ship with a permanently incorrect base structure
checksum (cc_base) baked into their EEPROM at manufacturing time.
Confirmed on hardware: the identification bytes (vendor name, part
number, revision) decode identically and consistently across
repeated reads, unbind/rebind cycles, and full power cycles -- only
the checksum byte itself is wrong. This is a defect in the module's
EEPROM content, not a flaky I2C read.

Since 8ae94629abaf ("net: sfp: consider fixed link modules using SFP")
sfp_sm_mod_probe() hard-fails module identification with -EINVAL on
checksum mismatch, refusing to bring the link up at all. The existing
Cotsworks quirk already establishes the precedent for tolerating a
known-bad-checksum vendor by downgrading dev_err()+EINVAL to a
dev_warn() and continuing with the (otherwise valid) parsed data.
Extend that same tolerance to this generic OEM DAC.

On LS1046A this specific module is in daily use in eth3's SFP+ cage
(sfp-xfi0). NXP's vendor ASK1 kernel (6.12.49) tolerates this same
checksum failure and brings the link up fine; only the switch to a
strict mainline drivers/net/phy/sfp.c on the ASK2/dpaa1 kernel
(6.18.x) exposed the difference.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/net/phy/sfp.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c b/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
index 16f64abf6..3c6f33b37 100644
--- a/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
@@ -2324,6 +2324,7 @@ static int sfp_sm_mod_probe(struct sfp *sfp, bool report)
 	bool cotsworks_sfbg;
 	unsigned int mask;
 	bool cotsworks;
+	bool bad_checksum_oem_dac;
 	u8 check;
 	int ret;
 
@@ -2378,6 +2379,16 @@ static int sfp_sm_mod_probe(struct sfp *sfp, bool report)
 	cotsworks = !memcmp(id.base.vendor_name, "COTSWORKS       ", 16);
 	cotsworks_sfbg = !memcmp(id.base.vendor_pn, "SFBG", 4);
 
+	/* Generic no-name "OEM" 10G passive DAC modules (vendor_pn
+	 * "SFP-H10GB-CU1M") ship with a permanently incorrect base
+	 * structure checksum baked into their EEPROM at manufacturing
+	 * time. The identification bytes themselves (vendor/PN/rev) are
+	 * consistently valid on every read; only cc_base is wrong. Treat
+	 * exactly like the existing Cotsworks tolerance below.
+	 */
+	bad_checksum_oem_dac = !memcmp(id.base.vendor_name, "OEM             ", 16) &&
+			       !memcmp(id.base.vendor_pn, "SFP-H10GB-CU1M  ", 16);
+
 	/* Cotsworks SFF module EEPROM do not always have valid phys_id,
 	 * phys_ext_id, and connector bytes.  Rewrite SFF EEPROM bytes if
 	 * Cotsworks PN matches and bytes are not correct.
@@ -2391,7 +2402,7 @@ static int sfp_sm_mod_probe(struct sfp *sfp, bool report)
 	/* Validate the checksum over the base structure */
 	check = sfp_check(&id.base, sizeof(id.base) - 1);
 	if (check != id.base.cc_base) {
-		if (cotsworks) {
+		if (cotsworks || bad_checksum_oem_dac) {
 			dev_warn(sfp->dev,
 				 "EEPROM base structure checksum failure (0x%02x != 0x%02x)\n",
 				 check, id.base.cc_base);
